 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that the High Priest is judged by 71?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "answered by Rav Adda bar Ahava; challenged in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.16a.10",
   "daf": "16a",
   "sentenceId": 9,
   "he": "וְלֹא אֶת כֹּהֵן גָּדוֹל. מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַב אַדָּא בַּר אַהֲבָה: דְּאָמַר קְרָא ״כׇּל הַדָּבָר הַגָּדֹל יָבִיאוּ אֵלֶיךָ״, דְּבָרָיו שֶׁל גָּדוֹל.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that «the High Priest» may be judged only by the Great Sanhedrin, consisting of seventy-one judges. The Gemara asks: «From where is this matter» derived? «Rav Adda bar Ahava says that the verse states: “They shall bring every great matter to you”» (Exodus 18:22). This means that Moses, or the Great Sanhedrin with seventy-one judges, which served the parallel role to that of Moses, adjudicates all «matters» relating «to a great one,» i.e., the High Priest."
  }
 ],
